@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# Applying maintenance mode
+
+`MaintenanceGuard` only rejects calls it is actually asked to guard. Register it
+once, application-wide, so it evaluates every HTTP request and every WebSocket
+message:
+
+```ts
+providers: [
+  MaintenanceModeService,
+  { provide: APP_GUARD, useClass: MaintenanceGuard },
+];
+```
+
+Registering it globally does **not** make every handler subject to maintenance
+mode — only handlers or classes decorated with `@Maintenance()` are ever
+turned away; everything else keeps working exactly as before.
+
+Mark the surfaces affected by a migration:
+
+```ts
+@Controller('reports')
+export class ReportsController {
+  @Maintenance()
+  @Get('export')
+  export() {
+    /* rejected while maintenance mode is on */
+  }
+}
+
+@WebSocketGateway()
+export class ReportsGateway {
+  @Maintenance()
+  @SubscribeMessage('reports:export')
+  handleExport() {
+    /* rejected while maintenance mode is on */
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`@Maintenance()` on a class marks every handler declared on it; `@Maintenance()`
+on a method marks only that one handler.
+
+Toggle the mode from anywhere the running application already has DI access —
+an admin-only controller action, an internal CLI command, a health check
+reacting to a migration signal — by injecting `MaintenanceModeService` and
+calling `enable()` / `disable()`. No redeploy is involved; the flag lives in
+memory for the lifetime of the process.
+
+Because `MaintenanceGuard` and `MaintenanceModeService` only depend on plain
+constructor arguments and the standard `ExecutionContext` shape, both are easy
+to unit test directly: construct a `MaintenanceModeService`, toggle it, build a
+`MaintenanceGuard` with it, and call `canActivate()` with a stub context object
+exposing `getHandler()` / `getClass()` — no HTTP server or socket connection
+required.
diff --git a/packages/common/maintenance/CODEMANIFEST b/packages/common/maintenance/CODEMANIFEST
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c56a5a0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/common/maintenance/CODEMANIFEST
@@ -0,0 +1,87 @@
+Annotations: |
+  Implements an opt-in "maintenance mode" extension point built entirely from
+  existing Nest primitives: metadata-based marking plus the guard mechanism.
+  It intentionally does not distinguish between transports — the same
+  `Maintenance` marker and the same `MaintenanceGuard` apply to HTTP
+  controllers/handlers and to WebSocket gateways/message handlers alike, since
+  both already run resolved guard instances through the identical
+  guards-consumer mechanism before invoking a handler's own logic.
+
+---
+
+"Maintenance() -> decorator: MethodDecorator & ClassDecorator":
+  location: maintenance.decorator.ts
+  annotations: |
+    Marks a single handler method, or an entire controller/gateway class, as
+    affected by maintenance mode. Carries no configuration of its own — it only
+    records, via the framework's standard metadata mechanism, that the target
+    should be turned away while maintenance mode is switched on. Applying it to
+    a class marks every handler declared on that class; applying it to a method
+    marks only that handler. Targets without this marker are never affected,
+    regardless of the maintenance mode state.
+
+"MaintenanceModeService()":
+  location: maintenance-mode.service.ts
+  annotations: |
+    Injectable, process-wide toggle for maintenance mode. Holds no reference to
+    any request, transport, or handler — it is a plain boolean flag that other
+    parts of the running application (an admin endpoint, an internal CLI command,
+    a startup hook) flip at runtime, without redeploying. `MaintenanceGuard`
+    reads the current state on every guarded invocation to decide whether marked
+    handlers should currently be turned away.
+  methods:
+    "enable()": |
+      Switch maintenance mode on. From this point, handlers marked with
+      `Maintenance` are turned away until maintenance mode is switched off
+      again.
+    "disable()": |
+      Switch maintenance mode off. Handlers marked with `Maintenance` resume
+      running normally.
+    "isEnabled() -> enabled:boolean": |
+      Report the current maintenance mode state.
+
+      `enabled`: reflects whether maintenance mode is currently switched on
+
+"MaintenanceGuard(maintenanceModeService: MaintenanceModeService)":
+  location: maintenance.guard.ts
+  annotations: |
+    A guard meant to be registered once, globally, for the whole application
+    (HTTP and WebSocket handlers alike) — not per-controller. It is the single
+    mechanism that turns `Maintenance` markers into an actual rejection, and it
+    is transport-agnostic: it only inspects the resolved handler and
+    controller/gateway class carried by its execution context argument, so it
+    behaves identically whether that context originated from an HTTP request or
+    a WebSocket message.
+
+    `maintenanceModeService`: `MaintenanceModeService` from this cell, consulted
+    on every call to read the current maintenance mode state
+  methods:
+    "canActivate(context: Object<string, any>) -> allowed:boolean": |
+      Decide whether the handler behind `context` may run.
+
+      `context`: wraps either an HTTP request or a WebSocket message and exposes
+      the handler function and its declaring controller/gateway class
+      `allowed`: whether the handler may proceed
+
+      Algorithm:
+      1. When maintenance mode is currently switched off, allow immediately —
+         no handler is ever affected while it is off, regardless of markers
+      2. Otherwise, look up the `Maintenance` marker directly on `context`'s
+         handler function; if absent, fall back to the marker on `context`'s
+         declaring controller/gateway class
+      3. When no marker is found on either, allow — unmarked handlers are never
+         affected by maintenance mode
+      4. When a marker is found, reject by throwing a clear, dedicated error
+         instead of returning a denial, so callers see why the call was turned
+         away rather than a generic forbidden result
+
+---
+
+Author: Goga
+CreatedAt: 14/09/26
+Description: |
+  Documents the maintenance-mode extension point: a `Maintenance` marker
+  decorator plus a transport-agnostic `MaintenanceGuard` that consults a
+  runtime-toggleable `MaintenanceModeService` to reject calls to marked HTTP
+  handlers and WebSocket message handlers alike, before their own logic runs,
+  while leaving unmarked handlers completely unaffected.
